# INSTALL REACTOS 0.4.16 ONTO A DISK THIS STAGE MAKES, AND PUBLISH IT.
#
# REACTOS IS A DIFFERENT MACHINE SHAPE FROM EVERY OTHER GUEST HERE, IN THREE WAYS AT ONCE:
#
#	32-bit only          the ISO is i386, and there is no amd64 ReactOS release
#	BIOS only            it boots from the MBR through SeaBIOS; there is no UEFI loader
#	no virtio-blk        its install disc ships no driver for it, so the disk is IDE
#
# SO THERE IS NO `-drive if=pflash` LINE AND NO OVMF, and the target is `-drive if=ide`. A walk that
# copied the UEFI arguments every other guest here starts with would fail at the firmware before
# ReactOS was ever read. `-machine pc` is i440fx, which is what the project's own QEMU page uses.
#
# EVERY ACTION IS A KEYSTROKE, AND THAT IS FORCED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.
#
# ReactOS fixes its own display mode at 800x600 - its "Setup the basic devices" page says
# `VESA Display (800x600x32)` - and the engine REFUSES a `click:` on any frame that is not 1920x1080:
#
#	error: click (508, 447) is a coordinate measured at 1920x1080 but the framebuffer is 800x600.
#	       A click cannot be rescaled the way a reading can
#
# MEASURED, with `-device VGA,xres=1920,yres=1080,vgamem_mb=32`: the guest still draws 800x600, so the
# frame cannot be talked up to the size a click needs. The wizard is fully keyboard-driven - Tab moves
# between the buttons, Return presses the focused one, Space toggles a checkbox - so this file uses no
# coordinates at all and the 800x600 frame stops mattering.
#
# EVERY PRESS IS FOLLOWED BY A GATE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE LESSON OF THIS FILE.
#
# MEASURED, on the version of this walk that pressed Return through the pages with only `settle:`
# between them: the FIRST Return never reached the guest - the language dialog had not taken focus -
# so every later press landed one page too early. `Down` then moved the focus to `Run ReactOS Live CD`
# and the next Return launched the LIVE DESKTOP, and the walk then waited forty attempts for
# `copying files` on a machine that was never installing anything. The screen it left behind was the
# live desktop with an `Install ReactOS` icon on it, which is a dead end with no way back.
#
# A `settle:` IS A GUESS ABOUT TIME AND A GATE IS A FACT ABOUT THE SCREEN. Each gate below names words
# that only its own page draws, so a press that did not register stops the walk on THAT page with the
# page's name in the log, instead of silently shifting every press after it.
walkthrough "reactos 0.4.16 - install to a fat partition on a whole disk"
resolution 1920x1080
recording_resolution 1920x1080

var out
  default out/reactos.qcow2
  from args.out

step "create the empty disk"
  disk
    file disk.qcow2
    size 2

step "boot the installer"
  boot
    machine "pc,accel=kvm"
    args [["-cpu", "Skylake-Client-v4"], ["-smp", "2"], ["-m", "2048"], ["-chardev", "socket,id=qga0,path=qga.sock,server=on,wait=off"], ["-device", "isa-serial,chardev=qga0"], ["-device", "VGA,xres=1920,yres=1080,vgamem_mb=32"], ["-device", "piix3-usb-uhci,id=uhci"], ["-device", "usb-tablet,bus=uhci.0"], ["-device", "usb-kbd,bus=uhci.0"], ["-drive", "file=reactos.iso,media=cdrom,readonly=on,if=none,id=media0"], ["-device", "ide-cd,drive=media0,bus=ide.1,bootindex=0"], ["-drive", "file=disk.qcow2,if=ide,index=0,media=disk,format=qcow2,id=target"], ["-netdev", "user,id=net0"], ["-device", "rtl8139,netdev=net0"]]
    timeout 900

# THE GATE IS ONE WORD THAT SURVIVES OCR, AND THREE EARLIER GATES DID NOT. MEASURED, on a page that
# was plainly on screen:
#
#	[[language]]         the instruction line is read as `+t your languag` - small text over the
#	                     wallpaper's gradient loses its last letters
#	[[reactos, livecd]]  the title is read as `Reactos Liveco` - the trailing `CD` is dropped
#	[[reactos]]          read every single time, in the title and in the version line
#
# `ReactOS` also appears in `ReactOS Version 0.4.16-x86` at the bottom right, which is drawn only
# after the guest has booted - so the one word is a witness rather than a coincidence.
step "the livecd language page is up"
  repeat
    do
      item
        wait 5
      item
        read
          name lang
    until
      source lang
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 800, 600]
      rect_at [800, 600]
      scale 1
      words [[reactos]]
    max 60

# THE LANGUAGE PAGE NEEDS A `Tab` BEFORE THE `Return`, AND THE PAIR HAS TO BE RETRIED.
#
# Its focus starts in the language listbox, so a bare Return goes to the list and not to the button:
# MEASURED locally, `ret` alone left the page exactly as it was. `tab` THEN `ret` advances.
#
# BUT A SINGLE PAIR IS NOT ENOUGH IN CI, AND THAT IS MEASURED TOO. On run 45463343490 the same walk
# - the same file that reaches step 26 on the pod - stopped here with
#
#	status: failed step 6/26 "the choose-what-to-do page is up"
#	error: ... none of "run reactos" in "Reactos Liveco -ox 1 WZ@® \ a Next. Cancel Reactos ..."
#
# so both keys had been lost on a page that was plainly on screen, and a walk that presses each key
# exactly once has no way back from that. The loop below is the way back: `tab` and `ret` again, and
# again, until the next page's own words appear.
#
# THE RE-RUN IS SAFE BECAUSE THE GATE IS THE PAGE, NOT A CLOCK. If the first pair did land, the very
# next look sees the choose-what-to-do page and the loop stops without another key - so the only
# presses that happen are the ones needed. The alternative, a longer `settle:`, is a guess about how
# slow a CI guest is, and the measurement above is what a wrong guess costs.
step "advance past the language page, retrying the pair"
  repeat
    do
      item
        input tab
      item
        input ret
      item
        settle 3
      item
        read
          name choose
    until
      source choose
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 800, 600]
      rect_at [800, 600]
      scale 1
      words [[run, reactos]]
    max 20

# `Run ReactOS Live CD` IS FOCUSED FIRST, so Install is one press down. MEASURED: a bare Return here
# opens the live desktop instead of the installer, which is a dead end.
step "choose install rather than the live cd"
  input down
  settle 2

step "press install"
  input ret
  settle 4

step "the setup wizard is up"
  repeat
    do
      item
        wait 4
      item
        read
          name wiz
    until
      source wiz
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 800, 600]
      rect_at [800, 600]
      scale 1
      words [[wizard]]
    max 30

step "next past the welcome page"
  input ret
  settle 4

step "the installation type page is up"
  repeat
    do
      item
        wait 4
      item
        read
          name itype
    until
      source itype
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 800, 600]
      rect_at [800, 600]
      scale 1
      words [[installation, type]]
    max 30

step "next past the installation type"
  input ret
  settle 4

step "the basic devices page is up"
  repeat
    do
      item
        wait 4
      item
        read
          name dev
    until
      source dev
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 800, 600]
      rect_at [800, 600]
      scale 1
      words [[keyboard]]
    max 30

step "next past the basic devices"
  input ret
  settle 6

step "the partition page is up"
  repeat
    do
      item
        wait 4
      item
        read
          name part
    until
      source part
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 800, 600]
      rect_at [800, 600]
      scale 1
      words [[unpartitioned]]
    max 30

# ONE RETURN DOES BOTH: the partition is created and the Format dialog opens on top of it.
step "create the partition, which opens the format dialog"
  input ret
  settle 5

step "the format dialog is up"
  repeat
    do
      item
        wait 3
      item
        read
          name fmt
    until
      source fmt
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 800, 600]
      rect_at [800, 600]
      scale 1
      words [[format]]
    max 20

step "accept the fat quick format"
  input ret
  settle 6

step "the summary page is up"
  repeat
    do
      item
        wait 4
      item
        read
          name summ
    until
      source summ
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 800, 600]
      rect_at [800, 600]
      scale 1
      words [[summary]]
    max 30

# THE SUMMARY PAGE'S ONLY CONTROL IS A LICENCE CHECKBOX, AND IT MUST BE TICKED BEFORE INSTALL IS
# ENABLED. Space toggles it; then Tab moves Back -> Install.
step "accept the alpha-stage warning"
  input spc
  settle 2

step "move to install"
  input tab
  settle 1

step "move to install, second press"
  input tab
  settle 1

step "begin copying files"
  input ret
  settle 10

step "the files are being copied"
  repeat
    do
      item
        wait 15
      item
        read
          name copy
    until
      source copy
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 800, 600]
      rect_at [800, 600]
      scale 1
      words [[copying, files]]
    max 60

# THE SECOND STAGE IS ONE LOOP, AND IT REPLACES TEN BLIND RETURNS THAT DID NOT WORK.
#
# MEASURED: the version of this file that sent a `ret` for each of the ten second-stage pages and then
# gated on `[[computer]]` PUBLISHED A DISK THAT STILL SHOWED THE SETUP WIZARD. Two separate mistakes
# produced that, and the second is the reason this loop exists:
#
#  1. "Welcome to the ReactOS Setup Wizard" is drawn by BOTH stages, so the gate that was meant to say
#     "the second stage has started" fired on the first stage's own welcome page - the ten Returns were
#     spent while the guest was still copying files, and were lost.
#  2. the page's body says "This wizard installs ReactOS on your **computer**", so a gate on
#     `[[computer]]` matched WHILE THE WIZARD WAS STILL ON SCREEN. The walk then reported the install
#     complete and published a disk sitting on page one of the second stage.
#
# A LOOP THAT PRESSES RETURN AND LOOKS FOR THE DESKTOP CANNOT MAKE EITHER MISTAKE. It does not care how
# many pages there are, it does not care when the guest reboots, and it stops only on a word the wizard
# never prints: the desktop's own icon labels.
#
# THE GATE TAKES TWO READINGS OF THOSE LABELS, BECAUSE THE SAME DESKTOP READS DIFFERENTLY ON DIFFERENT
# RUNS. MEASURED locally `Computer  My Network  Recycle Bin  Applications`; MEASURED in CI (run
# 45455212639, same disk) `My (c) Read Me - 4 Documents 4 - a My 5`. `Documents` survives in the second
# where `Recycle Bin` does not, so `words: [[recycle, bin], [documents]]` accepts either. A gate that
# named only one of them reported failure on a screen that was plainly the desktop.
#
# THE RETURNS THAT LAND DURING THE REBOOT ARE HARMLESS. FreeLoader's menu is a single entry with
# `ReactOS` preselected, so a Return there boots the only thing there is.
# THE SECOND STAGE IS ENTERED BEFORE ANY KEY IS PRESSED, AND THAT IS THE FIX FOR CI.
#
# MEASURED ON RUN 45466239309: the loop that pressed Return from the moment copying began spent its
# ninety attempts on the FIRST stage's closing page, `Restarting your Computer`, and ended with
#
#	error: none of "recycle bin" or "documents" in "{ 4 , 3 FB Reactos setup x Restarting your ..."
#
# The presses were landing on that page's `Cancel`, which raises `Abort installation?` - so every
# attempt made the walk further from the desktop it was waiting for. On the pod the same loop worked
# because the guest rebooted before the first Return arrived; in CI it was slower and the presses hit.
#
# SO THERE ARE TWO STEPS AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE POINT:
#
#   1. WAIT for a page that exists only in the second stage - `Personalize your Software`,
#      `Regional settings`, `Appearance` - pressing NOTHING. The first stage's own pages cannot match,
#      and neither can `Restarting your Computer`, so no key can land on `Cancel`.
#   2. THEN walk the wizard, where Return is the `Next` button on every page and cannot cancel.
#
# `wizard` IS THE FIRST SET FOR A REASON THAT WAS MEASURED THE HARD WAY. The second stage opens on
# `Welcome to the ReactOS Setup Wizard`, and THAT PAGE WAITS FOR A KEY - so a gate that only named the
# pages after it deadlocked: MEASURED on run 45470402029, the loop pressed nothing, the welcome page
# never advanced, and the gate sat out all ninety attempts on the very page it was waiting for:
#
#	error: none of "personalize" or "regional settings" or "appearance" or "recycle bin" or
#	       "documents" in "ReactoS Setup Welcome to the ReactOS Setup [3 | Wizard ..."
#
# `wizard` IS SAFE HERE AND WOULD NOT BE ELSEWHERE. The first stage also draws "Welcome to the ReactOS
# Setup Wizard", which is why an earlier gate on that word fired too early - but THIS gate runs after
# `the files are being copied`, so the first stage's welcome page is a hundred steps behind us and the
# only wizard on screen is the second stage's.
step "the second stage has started, so no key can land on the first stage's cancel"
  repeat
    do
      item
        wait 10
      item
        read
          name stage2
    until
      source stage2
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 800, 600]
      rect_at [800, 600]
      scale 1
      words [[wizard], [personalize], [regional, settings], [appearance], [recycle, bin], [documents]]
    max 90

# THE GATE NEEDS TWO FACTS, NOT ONE: THE DESKTOP IS UP **AND** THE WIZARD IS GONE.
#
# MEASURED, TWICE END TO END: with `words: [[recycle, bin], [documents]]` alone the install published a
# disk that booted straight back into `Welcome to the ReactOS Setup Wizard`, and the smoke leg failed
# four times on it:
#
#	see: 55 word(s): ReactoS Setup Welcome to the ReactOS Setup Wizard This wizard ...
#
# THE REASON IS THAT THE WIZARD IS A WINDOW OVER THE DESKTOP. The second-stage dialog is drawn on top
# of a wallpaper that already has `My Documents`, `Read Me` and the rest of the icon column down its
# left edge, so `documents` is readable WHILE THE WIZARD IS STILL OPEN - the loop stopped at the first
# page of the second stage, shut the machine down, and published a half-configured system.
#
# THE COMPARISON IS `mode: text` WITH `without:`, AND `says_without` IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE. MEASURED on
# run 45482198765, which is how this file learned the difference:
#
#	error: `mode: says_without` is not a measurement of a frame; the ones this build has are
#	       like, mean_between, sat_between, spread_between, text, contains, matches and equals
#
# - the frame path answers `text` and refuses the `says*` modes that the bound-reading path accepts. The
# `text` path DOES honour `without:`, which is the half this gate needs, and it was measured directly
# with a probe walk before being relied on here: `words: [reactos]` with `without: [wizard]` passed on
# the language page, where `reactos` is present and `wizard` is not.
#
# ReactOS's second-stage pages all carry `Setup` and `wizard` in their title bar; the installed desktop
# carries neither.
step "walk the second stage to the installed desktop"
  repeat
    do
      item
        input ret
      item
        settle 10
      item
        read
          name desk
    until
      source desk
      mode text
      rect [0, 0, 800, 600]
      rect_at [800, 600]
      scale 1
      words [documents]
      without [wizard, setup]
    max 90

# THIS FILE ENDS WITHOUT `save:`, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
#
# `save:` would publish the disk from inside the walk, and the engine refuses anything below 512 MiB
# (easydev-org/easyvm#9) - a complete ReactOS install is 415 MiB, so `save:` fails no matter what
# `min_mb` says. The install workflow that runs this file therefore stops the machine here and pushes
# `work/disk.qcow2` itself, with `scripts/publish-disk.py`, which has no floor.
#
# THE SHUTDOWN IS A POWER BUTTON, NOT A KILL, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN PREFERRED.
#
# MEASURED ON RUN 45474709018 -> 45475851476, the CI install published a 314 MB disk and the smoke leg
# then booted it straight back into `Welcome to the ReactOS Setup Wizard`:
#
#	see: 55 word(s): ReactoS Setup Welcome to the ReactOS Setup Wizard This wizard ...
#
# - the second stage had not been committed. The pod's copy of the same walk reached 415 MiB and boots
# to its desktop, and the difference between the two runs is WHEN the machine was killed: `kill` sends
# SIGKILL to qemu, so whatever the guest had written but not flushed is lost, and ReactOS's second
# stage writes its settings through a registry that is only consistent after it shuts down itself.
#
# `acpi power` asks the guest to shut down the way a user would, and `wait_until stopped` is what says
# it went: the same pair Haiku's install needed for the same reason (its BFS journal).
#
# THE RUN IS ALSO GIVEN A MOMENT TO SETTLE FIRST. The last step above stops as soon as the desktop's
# icon labels are readable, which is before a fresh login has finished writing its own profile.
step "let the new system finish writing its profile"
  settle 30

step "press the guest's power button"
  acpi power
  settle 5

step "wait for the guest to shut down, so the registry and the fat are committed"
  wait_until stopped
  timeout 180

# WHEN #9 IS FIXED this file gains `save "{{ out }}"` and the workflow loses its push step; the walk is
# otherwise unchanged.
